their  word for it then hanson's team set a new  furthest north on two sequential voyages  to the arctic the first one lasted for  five years between 1898 and 1902. this  time it was perry's turn to suffer the  wrath of the grim destroyer as henson  liked to call the merciless conditions  caused by mother nature in the arctic  wastelands the commander's feet froze so  badly that he lost nine toes frostbite  understandably this injury kept him on  the shelf for a while and henson's team  did not return to the arctic until 1905.  this trip was shorter as they had  obtained a state-of-the-art steamship  dubbed ss roosevelt which was powerful  enough to make its way through the  floating ice packs the climax of this  expedition was one of the rare occasions  when henson was not by the commander's  side as the team got separated earlier  during a storm this actually proved to  be a problem for peery as he didn't have  anyone with him skilled enough in  navigation to confirm that he had set a  new farthest north but they claimed it  anyway and this highlights their big  problem with these expeditions nowadays  robert perry has what we might call an  issue with credibility the guy made a  lot of bold claims and some of them  might not have been true we'll get to  the big one about reaching the north  pole in a minute but his claims of  setting new farthest north records have  also come under scrutiny these doubts  have also tarnished the accomplishments  of matthew henson by association  but enough of the farthest north  malarkey from now on it's north pole or  bust since this was the true goal that  matthew henson and all other arctic  explorers at the time had in mind in  1908 it was finally time for the big one  on july the 6th hanson set off on his  seventh and final arctic mission  alongside his longtime companion this  time determined to make it all the way  to the north pole they first traveled to  greenland where they picked up dozens of  inuit men including cooklatu who was  eager to go exploring alongside his  adoptive father as well as hundreds of  dogs and tons of meat from there they  sailed to ellsmere island in canada  where they made camp and left for the  pole in late february 1909. the simplest  tasks became complicated and exhausting  when they had to be done under the  brutal and relentless conditions at the  arctic even just soldering a few tin  cans became a job for a demon in hades  according to henson worked all day  soldering the tins of alcohol and a very  trying job it was i tried to work with  mittens on i tried to work with them off  as soon as my bare fingers would touch  the cold metal of the tins they would  freeze if i attempted to use my mittens  they would singe and burn and it was  impossible to hold the solder with my  bare skin gloves on when the expedition  started the party consisted of 24 men  but only six were present for the last  leg of the journey matthienson robert  perry and four inuit men six almost  turned to five when matthew henson  slipped on a block of ice and fell into  the icy cold water in the arctic such an  accident could easily have turned into a  death sentence in fact that is exactly  the outcome that befell one of henson's  compatriots professor ross g marvin who  drowned on this expedition while  returning back to camp henson however  did not share his fate as he was saved  by uttar one of the inuit men who  managed to grab the explorer by the name  of the neck just like a sled dog and  pull him out of the water they knew it  stripped hints of his wet clothes and  beat the water out of them then  following a wardrobe change the hunt for  the north pole resumed as if nothing had  ever happened that was when the  commander's nasty side started to show  itself because in that moment honesty  friendship and teamwork didn't matter as  much to him as becoming the first man to  reach the north pole he realized he knew  it weren't going to be a problem and he  would have left henson behind if he had  any chance of making it without him but  he didn't and he knew it matthew hansen  was the one who took care of the dogs  and the supplies he was also the one who  knew how to fix the sledges and he was  the only one who knew how to speak with  he knew it if anything henson was the  one who didn't need peri on april 6 1909  the team made camp for the last time on  their way to the pole up until this  point their camps were simply named camp  number one camp number two etc but they  felt that something special was needed  to mark this momentous occasion  therefore the commander took an american  silk flag and carefully planted it on  top of their igloo christening the  location camp morris k jessup after one  of their financiers the six man went to  sleep and woke up the next morning ready  to make a final march to the north pole  ostensibly the plan was for the faster  henson to scout ahead and then allow the  navy officer to overtake at the last  minute and claim the glory of being the  first to the north pole however henson's  team overshot their target let's say by  mistake but who knows so when perry  finally caught up to them matthienson  greeted him by saying i think i'm the  first man to sit on top of the world  according to henson's journal the mood  became decidedly frosty from that point  on even by arctic standards henson wrote  from the time of my arrival at the  roosevelt for nearly three weeks my days  were spent in complete idleness i would  catch a fleeting glimpse of commander  peri but not once in all that time did  he speak a word to me then he spoke to  me in the most ordinary matter-of-fact  way and ordered me to get to work not a  word about the north pole or anything  connected with it even though neither  one said anything about it they both  knew that the partnership they had had  for over 20 years had disintegrated  [Music]  and piri immediately went to work to  ensure that he was the one who enjoyed  all the fame and glory and not matthew  henson this did not prove difficult  despite his decades of experience and  success racial prejudices against henson  were still pervasive and the world at  large was more than happy to dismiss him  as simply a mere sidekick to the  commander even newspapers of the day  decried the fact that perry gave the  honor of reaching the north pole to a  black man instead of one of his white  companions such as the ship's captain  robert bartlett only other explorers who  knew matthienson personally understood  how indispensable he'd truly been to the  success of the mission and the peri  didn't take him by choice but because he  could not have done it without him even  after he published his own memoir in  1912 titled a negro explorer at the  north pole henson still went mostly  ignored however karma is you know what  and peary encountered a completely  different and unexpected opposition to  his claim frederick cook a man who  accompanied them on henson's first trip  to the arctic as the team's surgeon he  also claimed to have reached the north  pole and he said that he did it first  this triggered an intense debate over  who was telling the truth if indeed any  of them were whether by choice or by  force matthew henson mostly stayed out  of the controversy although he  disbelieved cook's story feeling that he  was too soft to succeed in such a  grueling mission as far as henson was  concerned he lived a quiet unassuming  life after his arctic days were over he  never tried to go on another mission  both because he did not want to do it  without his long-term companion and  because he felt that he'd already  accomplished what he set out to do in  the arctic henson found a job with the  u.s customs house in new york city on  recommendation from president teddy  roosevelt himself and he spent almost 30  years working there he had married again  to a woman named lucy ross in 1907 and  had an inuit concubine named akantinwa  while in greenland who gave him his only  biological child a son named anaok this  fact however was not publicized until  the 1950s after henson's death  unfortunately in the decades following  his historic trip to the north pole  matthiensen saw his accomplishments  swept under the rug and forgotten while  his former partner was made an admiral  and received multiple medals and honors  it wasn't until 1937 that henson was  made an honorary member of the explorers  club of new york and a decade later he  received a medal by the u.s navy for his  efforts mathienson's final tribute in  his lifetime came in 1954 when president  dwight eisenhower invited him to the  white house to receive a special  commendation for his work as an explorer  matthienson died a year later on march 9  1955 in the bronx age 88 in 1988  following a special exception granted by  president ronald reagan matthew henson  and his wife lucy were re-buried at the  arlington national cemetery with full  military honours the image showed henson  and the four men who accompanied him to  the frozen edge of the earth thus some  long deserved recognition was finally  granted to five intrepid explorers who  persevered and triumphed in the face of  adversity both from mother nature and  their fellow men  [Music]
 

